      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Dividing the base"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A bisector from the apex of a triangle splits the opposite side not in half but in the ratio of the other two sides. Let us see why this is useful."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "Thales theorem",
          "converse",
          "statement",
          "formula",
          "converse of bisector theorem",
          "applications"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Thales theorem: a line parallel to one side of a triangle divides the other two sides in the same ratio.\nConverse: if a line divides two sides in the same ratio, it is parallel to the third side.\nAngle bisector theorem: the internal bisector of an angle of a triangle divides the opposite side in the ratio of the sides containing the angle.\nIn triangle ABC with bisector AD, BD over DC equals AB over AC.\nThe converse is also true: if BD over DC equals AB over AC, then AD bisects angle A.\nUse it to find unknown lengths and to prove that bisectors meet at the incentre."
        }
      },
